    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ai-ranking">4. AI ranking</h2>
  <p>
    When AI ranking is on, Emploist uses an AI model to score each application against its job, based on the resume,
    the applicant's answers, and the job's details. AI ranking is on by default, and while your Plan is active, an admin
    can turn it off in Settings. On its own, AI ranking doesn't reject, advance, or email anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Match scores can be wrong, incomplete, or biased. They're a guide to which applications to review first, not a
    finding about whether someone is qualified. You decide whether and how to use AI ranking, and you're responsible for
    every hiring decision you make. If you use it, you must:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      have people review applications, and never base a decision about an applicant only on a match score, or skip an
      application because its score is low or missing
    </li>
    <li>
      tell applicants that AI helps review applications, and give any other notices and meet any other requirements that
      the law places on you for using AI in hiring. While AI ranking is on, the default careers page shows such a notice.
      If you use your own careers page code, add one yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      not use it to screen applicants on protected characteristics, such as race, religion, caste, sex, age, or
      disability, or on things that stand in for them
    </li>
    <li>
      not use it for anything other than reviewing applications for the job they were submitted to, such as evaluating
      or monitoring employees
    </li>
    <li>not use it, including through an AI assistant, to make decisions about applicants without a person's review</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To rank applications, we send them to our AI provider, named in our <a href="/privacy/#subprocessors"
      >list of subprocessors</a
    >. We may change our AI provider or model. We may limit or turn off AI ranking in some regions, or for some
    companies, where the law requires it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-assistants">5. AI assistants</h2>
  <p>
    Team members can connect AI assistants, such as Claude or ChatGPT, to Emploist through our MCP server. A connected
    assistant works as the person who connected it, with their role, and can do much of what that person can do in
    Emploist, such as reading and moving applications and editing jobs. AI assistants are on by default, and an admin
    can turn them off for your company in Settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI assistants are third-party services that you and your team members choose. They aren't part of Emploist, and
    their providers aren't our subprocessors. Customer Data an assistant receives is handled under the terms between its
    provider and you or your team member, and we don't control it. You're responsible for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>deciding which assistants your team may use, and making sure that sending Customer Data to them is lawful</li>
    <li>everything your team members' assistants do in Emploist, as if your team members did it themselves</li>
    <li>following <a href="#ai-ranking">section 4</a> when an assistant works with applications and match scores</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We may block or disconnect an assistant that we reasonably believe is insecure, harmful, or used against these Terms.</p>

